unit:PH a qudt:Unit ;
    rdfs:label "Acidity"@en ;
    dcterms:description """\r
  In chemistry the unit $\\textit{pH}$, also referred to as $\\textit{acidity}$ or $\\textit{basicity}$,\r
   is the negative logarithm (base 10) of the concentration of free protons (or hydronium ions).\r
  The definition of $pH$ in terms of hydrogen ions in solution is:\r
\r
  $$\\text{pH}=-\\log_{10}(a_{H^+})\\equiv-\\log_{10}\\left(\\left[H^+\\right]\\right)$$\r
\r
  Where $a_{H^+}$ is the equilibrium molar concentration of $H^+$ in the solution, the activity of\r
   the hydrogen ion in the solution.\r
  $$$$\r
  This definition is appropriate for concentrations equal to, or less than $1\\ mol/l$, \r
   where $aH+ \\equiv [H+]$, that is, $1\\ mol/L\\ HCl$ has a $pH$ of zero.\r
  $$$$\r
  To relate this to standard molality ($b^\\circ$), typically taken as $1 \\ mol/kg$, \r
   consideration is given to the activity ($a_{H^+}$) of the hydrogen ions.\r
  $$$$\r
  The activity can be expressed as:\r
\r
  $$a_{H^+} = \\gamma_{H^+} \\times m_{H^+}$$ \r
\r
  Where, $\\gamma_{H^+}$ is the activity coefficient, which adjusts the molality to account for\r
   non-ideal behavior due to interactions between ions in the solution.\r
  $m_{H^+}$ is the molality of hydrogen ions in the solution relative to the standard molality, \r
   expressed in $mol/kg$.\r
  $$$$\r
  The expansion of $pH$ then becomes:\r
\r
  $$\\text{pH} = -log_{10}\\left(m_{H+}\\times\\gamma_{H^+}\\right)$$\r
\r
  $$$$\r
  This definition is relevant in more concentrated solutions or when precise thermodynamic calculations are required. \r
  It reflects how the properties of the solution deviate from ideal behavior and provides a more accurate understanding of the $pH$ under various conditions.\r
  $$$$\r
  While $pH$ is a universally recognized scale for expressing hydrogen ion activity,\r
  its appropriateness and accuracy can diminish under conditions of extremely high\r
  ionic strength, non-aqueous environments, high temperatures, or very high or low $pH$ values.\r
  In such cases, alternative measurement strategies might be required to obtain meaningful and accurate descriptions of acidity or basicity.\r
  """^^qudt:LatexString ;
    qudt:applicableSystem sou:ASU,
        sou:CGS,
        sou:CGS-EMU,
        sou:CGS-ESU,
        sou:CGS-GAUSS,
        sou:IMPERIAL,
        sou:PLANCK,
        sou:SI ;
    qudt:hasDimensionVector qkdv:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1 ;
    qudt:hasQuantityKind qudtqk:Acidity,
        qudtqk:Basicity ;
    qudt:informativeReference "https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/IUPAC-GB3-2012-2ndPrinting-PDFsearchable.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    qudt:symbol "pH" ;
    qudt:ucumCode "[pH]"^^qudt:UCUMcs ;
    rdfs:comment "Unsure about dimensionality of pH; conversion requires a log function not just a multiplier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/2.1/vocab/unit> .

